doc.para:
Variables
　TJS2 variables are specified with the var keyword. In TJS2, variables cannot be used unless they are declared.
　The format of the var keyword is as follows:

　var VariableName;

　Multiple variable names can also be specified by separating them with , (comma). If an initial value is required for a variable,

　var VariableName = InitialValue;

　you can assign an initial value to the variable using this format.

	var variable=0;
	var var2=variable+2;
	var var3="this is var3.";
	var var1,var2,var3;
	var a=0,b=1,c=3;


　Variables do not need to have a type specified when declared. They take the type of the value assigned to them. Also, types are automatically converted during calculations or depending on the context.

Redeclaration of Variables
　Declaring a variable with the same name two or more times will not cause an error. However, if an initial value is specified in the second or subsequent declaration, that initial value will be assigned to the variable. If no initial value is given in the second or subsequent declaration, it becomes void.

	var a=0;
	var a; // Second declaration (does not cause an error, and a becomes void)
	var a=-1; // Third declaration (does not cause an error, but -1 is assigned to a)

Scope of Variables
　Variables declared in a section enclosed by { } (blocks) are variables valid only within that block, that is, local variables.
　Also, unlike C language, there are no restrictions on the positions where variables can be declared.

	while(t--)
	{
		var ratio=t/all; // Declare variable 'ratio'
		setRatio(ratio);
	}

	// When control moves here, 'ratio' has been deleted

	var test; // Variables can also be declared here


　Furthermore, if a variable with the same name as a variable outside the block is declared inside the block, only the newly declared variable can be accessed within that block.

	var ratio=0;
	if(flag)
	{
		var ratio; // Variable with the same name as the variable declared outside the block

		ratio=buf.position / buf.totalTime; // Only ratio inside the block can be accessed
		setRatio(ratio);
	}

	// When leaving the block, ratio inside the block is deleted, but ratio outside the block remains as is
	ratio=-1;


　If a variable is declared with var outside all scopes (this is called the global scope), that variable becomes a member of the global object.
